<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yerevan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Erevan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yerevan is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, on the Hrazdan river within sight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-ararat">Mount Ararat</a>. Its history begins with the Urartian fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erebuni">Erebuni</a>, founded in 782 BCE. Known as Erivan, it was the seat of an Iranian province and later of the Erivan Khanate until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> captured it in 1827. It became the capital of the first Armenian republic in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yerevan.jpg" alt="Yerevan" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Սէրուժ Ուրիշեան (Serouj Ourishian) · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Ararat_and_the_Yerevan_skyline_(June_2018).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A cuneiform inscription found in <b>Yerevan</b> records that the city was founded in the summer of 782 BCE by King Argishti I of Urartu, making the capital of the republic one of the world's oldest continuously inhabited cities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Yerevan</b>, capital of modern Armenia, traces its origin to the Urartian fortress of Erebuni, founded by Argishti I in 782 BCE, and celebrated its 2,750th anniversary in 1968.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>A second war in 1826–1828, despite early successes, ended in defeat and the Treaty of Turkmenchay, which surrendered <b>Erivan</b> and Nakhichevan and fixed the frontier on the Aras river.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Western Armenian took shape in Istanbul, where migrants from across the homeland brought their dialects together; Eastern Armenian, centred in Tbilisi, drew on the dialect of <b>Yerevan</b> and Mount Ararat.</blockquote><a href="/language/armenian">Armenian · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>An earthquake of magnitude about 6 struck around midnight and destroyed Dvin, the large commercial city that was the capital of early medieval Armenia, south of modern <b>Yerevan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-28">28 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Soviet rule brought industry, hydroelectric power at Lake Sevan, universities and the growth of <b>Yerevan</b> to a planned capital designed by Alexander Tamanian.</blockquote><a href="/state/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR</a></li></ul></section>
